    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. so if the ldl is building up in the arteries, does it like, actively damage them or is it just sitting there? i'm trying to understand how it actually affects the artery walls over time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL particles become trapped in the artery walls, and this accumulation, known as atherosclerosis, is the actual damaging process. Once trapped, the LDL can become oxidized and trigger an inflammatory response from your body. This inflammation then causes the plaque to build up, making the artery walls stiff and narrowing the passageway for blood. Over time, this narrowing restricts blood flow, which is what causes heart issues.",
      "source": "target",
